Expressing neonatal weight loss relative to birthweight allows standardized comparison across newborns with different birthweights and gestational ages.​[20]​ The days of the first and second postnatal weight assessments and the measurement day corresponding to maximal observed weight loss were also assessed. Physiological neonatal weight loss during the first postnatal days primarily reflects postnatal fluid adaptation and transitional feeding processes. Weight loss exceeding approximately 10% of birthweight is generally considered clinically relevant.​[29,30]​ 

Based on biological plausibility and literature indicating that physiological neonatal weight loss rarely exceeds 10–15% of birthweight, analyses were restricted to neonatal weight loss values below 20%. Observations outside this range were considered implausible and likely reflected  measurement errors in the historical records. 

Because the objective of this study was to characterize early neonatal weight loss, observations showing weight gain during the early postnatal period were considered unlikely to represent the physiological weight-loss phase ​[2]​ and were therefore excluded. Such observations most likely reflected weight measurements obtained after recovery of birthweight or documentation inconsistencies within the historical records. Overall, 397 observations (3.27%) were excluded because the recorded postnatal weight trajectories were inconsistent with the study objective of assessing early neonatal weight loss. These observations either showed weight loss (>20% of birthweight) (n=88, 0.73%) or weight gain (n=309, 2.55%) during the early postnatal period. Although only 3.27% of observations were excluded based on predefined biological plausibility criteria, excluded infants had somewhat less favourable neonatal characteristics than included infants. Therefore, a small degree of selection bias due to underrepresentation of clinically vulnerable newborns cannot be excluded. 

 

Feeding categories 

During the early twentieth century, artificial feeding differed substantially from modern infant formula feeding. Commercial infant formulas were not yet widely available, and artificial feeding typically consisted of animal milk (most commonly cow's milk), diluted milk preparations, or other milk substitutes prepared according to contemporary infant-feeding practices.​[31,32]​ Consequently, the historical feeding categories should not be interpreted as directly comparable with modern formula-feeding practices. 

The original feeding variable in the sources contained several detailed historical textual categories, including maternal feeding, artificial feeding, mixed feeding, and sequential changes between feeding types during hospitalization. To improve interpretability and avoid unstable estimates due to small category sizes, feeding information was recoded into broader categories. Newborns recorded in the source as receiving “maternal” milk were classified as breastfed. Categories indicating both maternal and non-maternal feeding were grouped as mixed feeding, whereas artificial feeding and maternal-to-artificial feeding were classified as artificial feeding. Rare or heterogeneous feeding categories, including records labelled as “human milk” and miscellaneous feeding descriptions, were grouped into an “other” category because of limited case numbers and uncertainty regarding the precise historical feeding practices represented by these records. The “other” category remained included in regression analyses but was interpreted cautiously because of its heterogeneity.  

Gestational age and birthweight 

Gestational age was assessed as categorical information at birth by the physician and could also be calculated based on the self-reported date of last menstruation and the date of delivery recorded in the source. If the date of last menstruation was missing, or if physician-assessed and calculated gestational age were discordant, gestational age as assessed by the physician at birth was used.​[26]​ Preterm birth was defined as gestational age <37 completed weeks. Birthweight was analysed both continuously and categorically, with low birth weight defined as birthweight <2500 g. 

Other neonatal and maternal variables 

The historical birth records also contained information on the mother's age (in years, as a continuous variable), the child's sex, and the parity of the birth. In addition, the sources also contained a wide range of qualitative information on the mothers' health status. Such information included assessments by the physician on maternal nutritional status (obesity, underweight), on the presence of a goitre, on maternal infections, and other clinical observations.  

A categorical variable called “General health status” was created based on this qualitative information, coded from 1 to 3, with higher numbers indicating poorer maternal health.​[25]​ For example, if there was none of these indications (obese, thin, goitre, rickets, infection), health status was = 1 (good). If there was albuminuria, eclampsia or other very severe/life threatening conditions being mentioned in this section of the records it was = 3 (very bad). Everything in between was coded as 2 (bad health status).  

Crisis exposure variables 

Pregnancy-related exposure to First World War and the influenza pandemic was determined using estimated conception dates derived from gestational age information. ​[20,25]​ Pregnancies were classified as exposed if any part of gestation overlapped with the First World War (July 1914–November 1918) or with the major influenza pandemic waves (July 1918–March 1919 and January 1920–March 1920). Information on maternal influenza-like illness during pregnancy was obtained from physician-recorded diagnoses documented in the original maternity records and was analysed as an additional exposure variable, as described in previous studies using the same historical records.​[25,26]​ 

 

Statistical analysis 

Descriptive analyses were performed to summarize maternal, neonatal, feeding-related, and obstetric characteristics of the study population. Continuous variables were described using means and standard deviations or medians and interquartile ranges, depending on data distribution. Categorical variables were summarized using absolute and relative frequencies. 

The primary outcome was maximum observed neonatal weight loss (%) during the early postnatal period. Associations between neonatal, maternal, and feeding-related factors and maximum observed neonatal weight loss were evaluated using multivariable linear regression models. Main models were adjusted for gestational age, because birthweight is part of the calculation of percentage neonatal weight loss and was therefore not included as an adjustment variable in the primary models. Gestational age is a major determinant of birthweight, and adjustment for both variables may lead to overadjustment or collider-stratification bias, particularly when estimating associations with neonatal outcomes.​[33]​ Therefore, separate model specifications were used: one adjusted for gestational age (main analyses)  and one adjusted for  birthweight (sensitivity analysis)  This approach allowed assessment of the robustness of the findings without conditioning simultaneously on two causally linked birth characteristics. All models included feeding category, historical exposure variables, neonatal sex, and continuous parity and maternal age as covariates. The secondary outcome was excessive neonatal weight loss as a binary variable, defined as weight loss exceeding 10% of birthweight. Associations with excessive neonatal weight loss were examined using multivariable logistic regression models. As for the linear analyses, gestational-age-adjusted models were considered the primary analyses, whereas birthweight-adjusted models were conducted as sensitivity analyses. 

To evaluate whether historical crisis exposure influenced neonatal weight loss, pregnancy-based exposure variables were included in the main multivariable regression models. Pregnancies were classified according to whether gestation overlapped with First World War and/or the influenza pandemic. These models examined whether neonatal weight loss differed according to maternal exposure to historical crises during pregnancy while accounting for feeding type and other covariates. To assess potential confounding by indication, whereby alternative feeding regimens may have been preferentially administered to clinically vulnerable mothers or newborns, maternal and neonatal characteristics were compared across feeding categories. Continuous variables were compared using Kruskal–Wallis tests, whereas categorical variables were compared using χ² tests or Fisher’s exact tests where appropriate. These analyses were performed to determine whether differences in baseline maternal or neonatal characteristics were associated with feeding practices. Because feeding practices may have differed according to maternal health and neonatal vulnerability, additional multivariable regression models were fitted including maternal health status as an additional covariate. Separate models further examined maternal influenza during pregnancy and maternal influenza occurring during pandemic-period pregnancies. These analyses were intended to assess the robustness of the observed feeding-related associations after accounting for indicators of maternal vulnerability and illness. 

Sensitivity analyses were performed by repeating all primary regression models with adjustment for birthweight instead of gestational age. This approach allowed assessment of the robustness of observed associations to alternative adjustment strategies for fetal growth and maturity.  

Assessment of effect modification: To investigate whether the association between breastfeeding and excessive neonatal weight loss differed across predefined subgroups, stratified analyses were performed according to prematurity status, low birthweight, neonatal sex, pregnancy exposure to the First World War, pregnancy exposure to the influenza pandemic, and maternal influenza during pregnancy. Separate logistic regression models were fitted within each stratum, and stratum-specific odds ratios for breastfeeding were extracted. Heterogeneity of breastfeeding associations across strata was formally assessed using Cochran–Q tests based on the stratum-specific odds ratios and their corresponding confidence intervals.  

For all analyses, statistical significance was defined as a two-sided p-value <0.05. Results are presented as regression coefficients (β) with 95% confidence intervals for linear models and odds ratios (ORs) with 95% confidence intervals for logistic models. 

All statistical analyses were performed using R statistical software (Version 4.3.2). Analytical code and study documentation are publicly available via GitHub.  

 

Results 

Study population characteristics 

A total of 13,033 historical birth records from the Lausanne Maternity Hospital were initially available for analysis. After excluding stillbirths (n = 536, 4.1%), records with missing live-birth status (n = 1, <0.1%), records with missing birthweight information (n = 8, 0.1%), and newborns without postnatal weight measurements (n = 352, 2.7%), 12,136 singleton live births remained eligible for analysis. Because the objective of this study was to characterize early neonatal weight loss, observations with weight trajectories that could not be reliably interpreted in relation to this objective were excluded according to predefined data-cleaning criteria. These observations either showed weight loss exceeding 20% of birthweight (n = 88, 0.73%) or weight gain during the available postnatal observation period (n = 309, 2.55%). Overall, 397 observations (3.27% of eligible singleton live births) met these exclusion criteria and were removed from the analytical dataset. The final analytical sample therefore comprised 11,739 singleton live births with available neonatal follow-up weight measurements, representing 90.1% of the initial study population (Figure 1). Characteristics of eligible live-born infants excluded because of non-evaluable weight trajectories are shown in Supplementary Table S1. Compared with infants included in the analytical cohort, excluded infants had lower mean birthweight (3031 g vs. 3225 g, p < 0.001) and lower mean gestational age (39.1 vs. 39.4 weeks, p < 0.001), and they were more frequently preterm (10.1% vs. 6.5%, p = 0.007) or low birthweight (15.4% vs. 6.3%, p < 0.001). Feeding mode also differed between included and excluded infants (p = 0.009). Maternal age, neonatal sex, and World War I exposure did not differ substantially between groups. 

Maternal, neonatal, and obstetric characteristics of the study population are summarized in Table 1. Mean maternal age was 28.1 years (SD 6.3), and 37.2% were first parities. Mean gestational age was 39.4 weeks (SD 1.6), indicating that the majority of newborns (93.5%) were delivered at term. Mean birthweight was 3,225g (SD=493g). Sex distribution was balanced, with 50.7% female and 49.2% male newborns. Breastfeeding represented the predominant feeding practice during the the postpartum period in the hospital, accounting for 77.9% of newborns, whereas 13.8% received mixed feeding and 6.4% artificial feeding.  

Timing of postnatal weight assessments  

Timing of postnatal weight assessments was relatively standardized across the historical maternity records. Although neonatal weight was commonly measured daily during the postnatal stay, the data collection framework predefined day 5 and day 10 after birth as the principal assessment time points for analysis. When measurements at these predefined time points were unavailable, the nearest documented weight measurement was used in a few occasions. The first transcribed postnatal weight assessment was therefore around day 5 after birth (median 5 days, range 1–10 days), whereas the second assessment was generally around day 10 (median 10 days, range 5–20 days). Maximal neonatal weight loss (in percent) was identified during the first postnatal assessment in 86.6% of newborns, whereas 13.4% reached their maximal recorded weight loss during the second assessment.  

Distribution of neonatal weight loss 

Maximum observed neonatal weight loss (as expressed as percentage of birthweight) during the first days of life showed interindividual variability. Mean neonatal weight loss was 6.7% (SD 3.5). The distribution of maximum observed neonatal weight loss is shown in Figure 2. Overall, 1,971 newborns (16.8%) experienced excessive neonatal weight loss exceeding 10% of birthweight. Weight loss differed substantially according to feeding type (Figure 3): Breastfed newborns demonstrated the lowest mean weight loss (average 6.3%, SD 3.4), whereas artificially fed newborns showed a mean weight loss of 7.5% (SD 3.6). Similarly, excessive weight loss occurred in 13.5% of breastfed newborns, compared with 23.1% of artificially fed newborns and 30.2% of mixed-fed newborns. In descriptive analyses by historical period, average weight loss was 6.5% during the pre-war period, 7.0% during the First World War, 7.1% during the influenza/post-war period, and 6.6% during the post-crisis period. The highest mean neonatal weight loss was observed during the influenza/post-war period, whereas values during the post-crisis years returned to levels comparable to those observed before the First World War (Figures S2 and S5). Likewise, the proportion of neonates experiencing excessive weight loss (>10%) appeared higher during the First World War and influenza/post-war periods than during the pre-war and post-crisis periods.  

Main multivariable analyses of neonatal weight loss (Gestational-age-adjusted) 

In the main gestational-age-adjusted linear regression model, feeding mode was strongly associated with maximum observed neonatal weight loss (Table 2). Compared with artificially fed newborns, breastfed newborns had substantially lower maximum weight loss (β = −1.07 percentage points, 95% CI −1.32 to −0.81), whereas mixed feeding was associated with higher maximum weight loss (β = 0.94 percentage points, 95% CI 0.65 to 1.23), after adjustment for gestational age, maternal age, parity, neonatal sex, and historical exposure variables. Compared with pregnancies not overlapping the respective exposure period, pregnancy overlapping the influenza pandemic was associated with greater neonatal weight loss (β = 0.44 percentage points, 95% CI 0.27 to 0.60), whereas pregnancy overlapping the First World War showed only a small and imprecise association (β = 0.11 percentage points, 95% CI −0.03 to 0.25). Male and female newborns had similar maximum observed neonatal weight loss in the main model. In the corresponding gestational-age-adjusted logistic regression model for excessive neonatal weight loss >10% (Table 3), breastfeeding was associated with substantially lower odds of excessive weight loss compared with artificial feeding (OR = 0.56, 95% CI 0.47 to 0.68). In contrast, mixed feeding was associated with higher odds of excessive weight loss (OR = 1.52, 95% CI 1.24 to 1.87), as was the “other” feeding category (OR = 1.45, 95% CI 1.00 to 2.09). 

Overall, feeding mode showed by far the strongest association with neonatal weight loss. Pregnancy overlapping the influenza pandemic was associated with higher maximum observed neonatal weight loss compared with pregnancies not overlapping the influenza pandemic, whereas evidence for an association with excessive neonatal weight loss was weaker. There was no clear evidence that the odds of excessive neonatal weight loss differed by neonatal sex in the main gestational-age-adjusted model (OR = 0.92, 95% CI 0.83 to 1.02) (Figure 4). 

Maternal and neonatal characteristics differed significantly across feeding categories (Supplementary Table S2). Breastfeeding was generally more common among newborns with more favourable maternal and neonatal characteristics, whereas artificial and mixed feeding were more frequently observed among clinically vulnerable mother–infant pairs. Artificially fed newborns had lower mean gestational age (39.1 vs. 39.5 weeks, p < 0.001) and lower mean birthweight (3127 vs. 3238 g, p < 0.001) than breastfed newborns. Consistent with these differences, low birthweight and preterm birth were more common among artificially fed infants (11.3% and 11.1%, respectively) than among breastfed infants (5.3% and 5.7%, respectively) (all p < 0.001). Mothers of artificially fed and mixed-fed newborns were older and had higher parity than mothers who breastfed. Poor maternal health status was more common among artificially fed and mixed-fed infants than among breastfed infants (all p < 0.001). Maternal influenza during pregnancy and maternal influenza occurring during the pandemic period were also unevenly distributed across feeding categories. Both exposures were more frequent among mixed-fed and other-fed newborns than among breastfed or artificially fed newborns (all p < 0.001). In contrast, neonatal sex distribution did not differ substantially according to feeding mode (p = 0.309). 

Maternal influenza and pandemic-related exposure analyses 

Additional analyses examined whether maternal influenza exposure during pregnancy was associated with neonatal weight loss independently of feeding mode and other covariates. Maternal influenza during pregnancy was associated with an average increase of 0.42 percentage points in maximum observed neonatal weight loss (95% CI 0.03 to 0.82) compared with pregnancies in which the mother did not have documented influenza during pregnancy (Supplementary Table S3). Despite inclusion of maternal influenza in the model, the associations between feeding mode and neonatal weight loss remained largely unchanged. Compared with artificial feeding, breastfeeding was associated with 1.06 percentage points lower neonatal weight loss (95% CI −1.32 to −0.81), whereas mixed feeding was associated with 0.96 percentage points higher neonatal weight loss (95% CI 0.67 to 1.26). A second model evaluated a combined exposure variable representing maternal influenza during pregnancy occurring within the influenza pandemic period (Supplementary Table S4). This exposure showed a similar association with an estimated increase of 0.41 percentage points in neonatal weight loss (95% CI 0.00 to 0.82). Overall, these findings suggest that maternal influenza exposure was not a strong confounder of the association between feeding mode and neonatal weight loss, but that it was associated with greater neonatal weight loss. 

Sensitivity tests, stratified analyses, and effect modification  

Sensitivity analyses were performed using birthweight adjustment instead of gestational age adjustment. Overall, results were highly consistent with those observed in the main gestational-age-adjusted analyses (Supplementary Tables S5 and S6). These findings indicate that the observed associations between feeding mode, pandemic exposure, and neonatal weight loss were robust to alternative adjustment strategies. 

Furthermore, stratified analyses were performed to evaluate whether the association between breastfeeding and excessive neonatal weight loss differed according to neonatal vulnerability (only term newborns and normal birthweight), sex, or historical crisis exposure status (Supplementary Table S7).  

Across most strata examined, breastfeeding was associated with lower odds of excessive neonatal weight loss compared with artificial feeding, although estimates were less precise among preterm and low-birthweight newborns. Among term newborns, breastfeeding was associated with lower odds of excessive neonatal weight loss (OR = 0.53, 95% CI 0.44–0.65) compared with artificial feeding, while the corresponding estimate among preterm newborns was less precise (OR = 0.75, 95% CI 0.45–1.26). No evidence of heterogeneity between term and preterm strata was observed according to the Cochran–Q test (p = 0.239). Comparable findings were observed when analyses were stratified by low birthweight status. Breastfeeding was associated with lower odds of excessive neonatal weight loss among normal-birthweight newborns (OR = 0.53, 95% CI 0.43–0.64), whereas the estimate among low-birthweight newborns was imprecise (OR = 0.91, 95% CI 0.53–1.57). The protective association of breastfeeding was also observed irrespective of historical exposure status (Supplementary Figure S1). Breastfeeding was associated with lower odds of excessive neonatal weight loss among pregnancies occurring both outside and during the influenza pandemic, as well as among pregnancies occurring both outside and during the First World War. Similar findings were observed when analyses were stratified according to maternal influenza during pandemic-period pregnancy and by neonatal sex. Overall, the association between breastfeeding and lower odds of excessive neonatal weight loss was consistent across the examined neonatal subgroups and historical exposure periods. 

Discussion 

In this historical cohort of 11,739 neonates born between 1905 and 1924, feeding mode emerged as the strongest determinant of neonatal weight loss. Breastfeeding was consistently associated with lower maximum observed neonatal weight loss and lower odds of excessive weight loss, whereas mixed feeding was associated with higher weight loss across all analyses. These associations remained highly consistent across the main gestational-age-adjusted models and birthweight-adjusted sensitivity models, indicating that feeding-related effects were robust to alternative modelling strategies. In addition to feeding mode, maternal influenza exposure and pregnancies overlapping the 1918–1920 influenza pandemic were associated with increased neonatal weight loss. By contrast, no independent association between First World War exposure and neonatal weight loss remained after adjustment for feeding mode and perinatal characteristics. However, the magnitude of the feeding-related associations substantially exceeded that of the historical crisis-related exposures, highlighting feeding mode as the dominant correlate of neonatal weight loss in this cohort. 

Feeding mode and neonatal weight loss 

Breastfeeding was consistently associated with lower neonatal weight loss and lower odds of excessive neonatal weight loss, whereas mixed feeding was associated with greater neonatal weight loss. The strong association between feeding mode and neonatal weight loss is broadly consistent with contemporary neonatal literature, which identifies infant feeding as one of the most important determinants of early postnatal weight trajectories. Several modern studies have reported significant associations between feeding practices and both physiological and excessive neonatal weight loss.​[2–6,34]​ However, the direction of the association observed in the present study differs from that reported in many contemporary cohorts. Modern studies frequently describe greater early physiological weight loss among exclusively breastfed newborns, particularly during the establishment of lactation and the transition to effective milk transfer.​[2–6]​ In contrast, breastfed infants in our historical cohort experienced lower neonatal weight loss than artificially fed infants. From a human-biological perspective, lactation represents an important component of maternal investment, marking the transition from prenatal placental provisioning to postnatal transfer of maternal resources to the offspring.​[9,35,36]​  

 

One possible explanation is that artificial feeding practices in the early twentieth century differed substantially from contemporary formula feeding. ​[31,32]​ Artificial feeding may not have provided the same nutritional support as modern infant formula and may therefore have been associated with less favourable early postnatal weight trajectories. Artificial feeding practices in the early twentieth century differed substantially from those of today.​[31,32]​ Commercial infant formulas were not yet widely available, and artificial feeding generally consisted of animal milk or other milk substitutes prepared according to contemporary infant-feeding practices. Consequently, feeding categories recorded in historical maternity records should not be assumed to be directly comparable with contemporary infant feeding practices.  

Taken together, these findings suggest that feeding mode played a central role in shaping neonatal weight trajectories in this historical population. They are compatible with a broader human-biological interpretation in which the establishment of postnatal feeding forms part of the transition from prenatal maternal provisioning to postnatal nutritional dependence. The observed association between breastfeeding and lower neonatal weight loss highlights the importance of interpreting infant feeding within its specific historical context and suggests that the relationship between feeding mode and neonatal weight loss may have differed from that observed in modern clinical settings. 

Feeding practices and confounding by indication 

An important consideration when interpreting the observed feeding-related associations is that feeding mode was unlikely to be randomly distributed within the cohort. Artificially fed infants had lower birthweight, lower gestational age, and higher frequencies of low birthweight and prematurity than breastfed infants. In addition, poorer maternal health was more common among mothers whose infants received artificial or mixed feeding. These findings suggest that feeding mode may have reflected underlying maternal and neonatal vulnerability in addition to nutritional exposure itself.  

These patterns can also be considered within the maternal capital framework, in which aspects of maternal phenotype and condition represent resources available for investment in offspring.​[8,9,36]​ Maternal condition may therefore be related both to postnatal maternal investment and to offspring condition, potentially contributing to the observed relationships between maternal vulnerability, feeding practices, and neonatal outcomes. Importantly, the variables available in our historical records do not constitute a direct measure of maternal capital; this framework is therefore used as an interpretive perspective rather than as a mechanism directly tested in the present study. 

This interpretation is consistent with historical infant feeding practices. Breastfeeding remained the dominant mode of infant feeding throughout much of Europe during the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, whereas artificial feeding was often introduced when breastfeeding was not possible because of maternal illness, lactation difficulties, neonatal weakness, or other medical complications. Artificial feeding therefore frequently represented a response to adverse maternal or neonatal circumstances rather than a purely elective feeding choice.​[31,32]​ Consequently, feeding mode may have acted as both a nutritional exposure and a marker of vulnerability. Infants who were already at increased biological risk may have been more likely to receive artificial or mixed feeding and may also have been more susceptible to greater postnatal weight loss. Confounding by indication  therefore remains a plausible explanation for part of the observed association between feeding mode and neonatal weight loss. However, the associations between feeding mode and neonatal weight loss remained evident after adjustment for maternal health status and across alternative models adjusting for either gestational age or birthweight, and other relevant covariates, and in analyses stratified by preterm birth or low birthweight status. Although residual confounding cannot be excluded, these findings suggest that differences in vulnerability alone are unlikely to fully explain the observed associations. Taken together, feeding mode in this historical cohort likely reflected both infant nutrition and underlying maternal-neonatal vulnerability. The observed associations should therefore be interpreted as the combined result of nutritional and clinical factors rather than as purely causal effects of feeding type. 

Prenatal adversity, maternal influenza and neonatal adaptation 

In addition to feeding-related factors, maternal influenza exposure during pregnancy and pregnancies overlapping the 1918–1920 influenza pandemic were associated with increased neonatal weight loss. Although these associations were smaller than those observed for feeding mode, they suggest that prenatal exposures may have contributed to variation in early neonatal weight trajectories. These findings are compatible with developmental plasticity frameworks, in which adverse prenatal environments may influence fetal growth and physiological development, with potential consequences extending into the neonatal period.​[8]​  Previous studies have demonstrated associations between maternal infection, nutritional deprivation, and altered fetal growth, birth outcomes, and later-life health.​[10,11,16]​  

 

Historical studies of prenatal adversity, including the Dutch Hunger Winter and the 1918 influenza pandemic, have further shown that gestational exposures may have lasting effects on offspring health and development.​[7,10,16]​ Several biological mechanisms may explain the observed associations. Maternal influenza infection may influence fetal development through inflammatory pathways, placental dysfunction, altered nutrient transfer, or impaired fetal growth.​[37]​ Such processes could affect neonatal physiological adaptation after birth and potentially contribute to greater postnatal weight loss. More broadly, pregnancies occurring during the influenza pandemic may have been exposed not only to infectious disease but also to nutritional, social, and psychological stressors that could have influenced fetal development and neonatal adaptation.  

Our findings are also consistent with recent historical studies from Switzerland. Le Vu et al. reported associations between maternal influenza-like illness and adverse neonatal outcomes during the 1918 pandemic using the same dataset, while additional work has suggested that prenatal infectious exposures may influence fetal growth patterns and neonatal development.​[25,26]​ The present findings extend this literature by suggesting that prenatal infectious exposures may also be associated with early postnatal weight trajectories. Nevertheless, the observational nature of the study precludes causal inference. Because maternal influenza was identified from physician-recorded diagnoses, the observed association should be interpreted cautiously, as incomplete confirmation of influenza cases cannot be excluded. The observed associations should therefore be interpreted as compatible with developmental origins hypotheses rather than as direct evidence of causal prenatal programming. Taken together, these findings suggest that prenatal infectious exposures may have contributed to altered neonatal adaptation, reflected by increased neonatal weight loss during the early postnatal period. 

Historical crisis periods: influenza pandemic versus World War I 

 Previous historical studies  from Switzerland and other European settings have linked wartime conditions, food shortages, and infectious disease outbreaks to changes in birthweight, placental characteristics, and other indicators of neonatal health.​[14,15,19–21]​ Similarly, studies using historical maternity records have reported adverse effects of the First World War and related societal disruptions on neonatal anthropometric outcomes.​[19–21]​ The present findings only partially align with this literature. While pregnancies occurring during the influenza pandemic showed increased neonatal weight loss, there was no clear independent association with pregnancy overlap with the First World War . One possible explanation is that pandemic-related exposures exerted more direct biological effects through maternal infection and inflammation, whereas wartime influences were likely mediated through broader social and nutritional pathways.  

The historical context of Switzerland may also be relevant when interpreting these findings. Although the country experienced substantial economic disruption, food insecurity, and social strain during the First World War, it remained militarily neutral and was not exposed to the widespread destruction experienced in many neighbouring countries.​[12,14,15]​ Consequently, wartime conditions may have been insufficient to produce an independent effect on neonatal weight loss once maternal, neonatal, and feeding-related factors were taken into account. An additional consideration is that pandemic-period exposure may represent a composite marker encompassing infectious, nutritional, social, and psychological stressors occurring simultaneously during the late war and immediate post-war years. From a human-biological perspective, the pandemic period may therefore be better interpreted as a broader ecological stress context rather than as a proxy for influenza infection alone. The observed associations should therefore not be interpreted as reflecting the biological effects of influenza infection alone. Taken together, these findings suggest that pandemic-related exposures were more strongly associated with neonatal weight loss than World War I exposure itself, highlighting the potential importance of infectious and societal stressors for neonatal adaptation in historical populations. 

Neonatal weight loss as a marker of neonatal adaptation in historical populations 

Neonatal weight loss may be conceptualized as a short-term phenotype occurring at the transition between prenatal development and postnatal nutritional provisioning. It may therefore reflect aspects of neonatal physiological adaptation after birth while also being influenced by the establishment and mode of postnatal feeding. In historical populations, neonatal weight trajectories may provide information complementary to conventional birth anthropometry by capturing aspects of adaptation occurring after delivery. The protective association between breastfeeding and lower neonatal weight loss was observed consistently across all examined neonatal subgroups. Cochran–Q heterogeneity tests provided no evidence that the association between breastfeeding and excessive neonatal weight loss differed across prematurity status, birthweight category, neonatal sex, historical crisis exposure, or maternal influenza exposure during pregnancy. These findings suggest that the association between feeding mode and neonatal weight loss was broadly similar across clinically and historically vulnerable neonatal populations. Nevertheless, the clinical interpretation of proportional weight loss may differ between neonatal populations. Similar percentage weight losses may impose a greater physiological burden in vulnerable infants, particularly those born preterm or with low birthweight, because of their lower metabolic reserves and reduced physiological capacity to compensate for postnatal nutritional deficits. Consequently, statistical consistency across subgroups should not necessarily be interpreted as implying equal clinical relevance across all neonatal populations. Taken together, these findings suggest that the association between breastfeeding and lower neonatal weight loss was broadly consistent across vulnerable neonatal populations, further supporting the robustness of the observed feeding-related effects. 

Strengths and limitations 

This study has several strengths. First, it is based on a large historical cohort comprising more than 11,000 neonates with serial weight measurements, allowing detailed assessment of early neonatal weight trajectories. Second, the availability of detailed feeding information enabled evaluation of feeding-related determinants of neonatal weight loss in a historical setting. Third, the study was conducted within a unique period encompassing both the First World War and the 1918–1920 influenza pandemic, providing an opportunity to investigate the influence of major societal and infectious stressors on neonatal outcomes. In addition, the availability of maternal, neonatal, and perinatal information allowed adjustment for a broad range of potential confounders. Finally, the use of multiple sensitivity analyses strengthened the robustness of the findings. 

Several limitations should also be considered. The observational design precludes causal inference and leaves the possibility of residual confounding. In particular, confounding by indication may have influenced the observed associations between feeding mode and neonatal weight loss, as feeding allocation was unlikely to be random. Furthermore, the use of historical hospital records inevitably entails limitations related to data completeness, measurement accuracy, and potential misclassification of exposures and outcomes. Postnatal weight measurements were primarily available around days 5 and 10 rather than continuously across the first postnatal days. Consequently, the true postnatal weight nadir may not have been captured in all infants, and the maximum observed weight loss should be interpreted as an approximation of maximal early neonatal weight loss. In some infants, particularly those with later measurements, weight recovery may already have begun by the time of assessment. 

Observations with non-evaluable weight trajectories were excluded based on predefined biological plausibility criteria. Although these exclusions represented only a small proportion of eligible live-born infants, excluded infants were more likely to have indicators of neonatal vulnerability, including lower birthweight, lower gestational age, preterm birth, and low birthweight. This may have led to a slight underrepresentation of clinically vulnerable newborns in the analytical cohort. Information on feeding frequency, milk volume, and actual nutritional intake was unavailable, limiting detailed assessment of feeding practices and preventing evaluation of dose-dependent feeding effects. In addition, the historical records did not provide direct measures of maternal nutritional intake, energy balance, milk production or composition, placental function, or other physiological components relevant to maternal capital. Consequently, the maternal-investment and developmental-plasticity frameworks used in this study should be considered interpretive perspectives rather than mechanisms directly tested in the present analyses. In addition, maternal influenza exposure was likely underestimated since only clinically documented cases could be identified. Finally, the cohort originated from a single maternity hospital, which may limit the generalisability of the findings to other historical populations, geographical settings, or healthcare systems. 

 

Conclusion 

In this historical cohort of neonates born between 1905 and 1924, feeding mode emerged as the strongest correlate of neonatal weight loss. Breastfeeding was consistently associated with lower neonatal weight loss, whereas mixed feeding was associated with greater weight loss across multiple analytical approaches. In addition, maternal influenza exposure and pregnancies occurring during the 1918–1920 influenza pandemic were associated with modest increases in neonatal weight loss, while no effect identified for the exposure to the First World War. 

These findings suggest that early neonatal weight trajectories may represent a postnatal phenotype at the intersection of infant physiological adaptation, postnatal nutritional provisioning, and prenatal environmental conditions. Beyond its established clinical relevance, neonatal weight loss may therefore provide information complementary to traditional measures such as birthweight and neonatal anthropometry by capturing aspects of adaptation occurring after delivery. Historical maternity records offer a unique opportunity to investigate how maternal, nutritional, infectious, and broader environmental conditions shape neonatal adaptation during periods of major societal disruption.
 
 

